  "summary": "West Africa's ancient ideographs: thousands of signs known to Leopard Society initiates alone.",
  "description": "Nsibidi is one of Africa's oldest indigenous scripts — thousands of ideographic signs cut into calabashes, tattooed on bodies, drawn on the ground and painted on ukara cloth, encoding court judgments, love messages and Leopard Society (Ekpe) secrets. Public signs are known to all; graded knowledge belongs to initiates, which made the script simultaneously writing and institution. Colonial officials documented fragments; the Ekpe society keeps parts of the system in use, and ukara initiation cloths still carry dense nsibidi panels today.",
